  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 PM MDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nSatellite shows a mix of clearing and cloud cover across our CWA so \nfar today. The cold front is likely hung up in the Central Mountains \nat the moment, evidenced in part by the dew points there in the low \nto mid 40s versus the dew points in the mid to upper 50s in the \neastern half of the area. We will see how this clearing along the \nSnake River Plain allows for convection to develop this afternoon. \nThe \"Slight Risk\" from SPC has been pushed a bit farther south out \nof Idaho and into Utah, but we remain under a \"Marginal Risk\" from \nBlackfoot south into Utah. Wind is still the main threat with the \npotential for gusts of 50-60+ mph, but hail with some storms could \nalso be pushing quarter size or larger. CAMs show another round of \nshowers and embedded storms early Sunday morning through parts of \nthe Snake River Plain and Eastern Highlands as the cold front \nfinally makes it through the area. \n\nTemperatures are a few degrees cooler today, in the mid to upper 70s \nin the eastern Magic Valley and Snake River Plain. Highs are \nexpected to remain in the 60s and 70s area wide on Sunday behind the \ncold front. Winds this afternoon and Sunday afternoon are going to \nbe a bit more gusty, ranging from 25 to 35 mph. This pushes us close \nto Lake Wind Advisory criteria early Sunday morning, but it appears \nsustained winds will diminish a bit by mid morning around the \nAmerican Falls Reservoir, so will hold off on issuing. \n\nWe will keep around some isolated shower/thunderstorm chances on \nMonday into Tuesday as a weak shortwave trough passes through the \narea. Much of next week remains under the influence of general \ntroughing over the West before another deep low moves into the PNW \non Friday. For the most part, afternoon highs remain in the upper \n70s to low 80s for most of the week ahead with overnight \ntemperatures in the 40s and 50s.",
